Repair the complete CommonJS source below and return a corrected complete source file exporting exactly `{ encodeRoute }`.

`encodeRoute(segments)` accepts an array containing only strings. It must encode each segment exactly once with `encodeURIComponent` and join the results with `/`, prefixed by one leading `/`. An empty array returns `/`; empty segments are preserved, so `["a", ""]` returns `/a/`. Input order must be retained. Non-arrays or non-string elements throw `TypeError`. If native encoding encounters malformed surrogate data, its `URIError` is allowed to propagate. The function must not mutate or alias the input.

Known-bad source:
```javascript
'use strict';

function encodeRoute(segments) {
  if (!Array.isArray(segments) || !segments.every((part) => typeof part === 'string')) {
    throw new TypeError('Segments must be strings');
  }
  const encoded = segments.map((part) => encodeURIComponent(encodeURIComponent(part)));
  return encoded.length === 0 ? '/' : '/' + encoded.join('/');
}

module.exports = { encodeRoute };
```

